Why Is Demand Increasing?
Increasing cancer incidence: Rising cancer diagnoses worldwide are creating sustained demand for molecular profiling and precision-oncology testing.
Growing precision-medicine adoption: Healthcare providers are increasingly using genomic information to identify actionable mutations and guide targeted therapies.
Declining sequencing costs: Advances in sequencing chemistry, automation and workflow design are reducing per-sample costs and expanding access.
Expansion of liquid biopsy: Circulating tumor DNA testing is creating new opportunities for minimally invasive disease monitoring.
AI-powered bioinformatics: Artificial intelligence is improving variant calling, annotation and clinical interpretation while reducing turnaround times.
Clinical Genomics & Precision Oncology Watch
Clinical Oncology NGS is moving from specialized genomic testing toward broader routine use across hospitals, specialty laboratories and oncology-care pathways. Falling reagent costs and improvements in sequencing throughput are helping make comprehensive molecular profiling more accessible to community hospitals and mid-sized laboratories.
Liquid-biopsy applications are becoming particularly important because they allow clinicians to monitor treatment response and resistance mutations using minimally invasive samples. At the same time, AI-enhanced bioinformatics is helping transform large sequencing datasets into clinically actionable reports. Companies that combine sequencing platforms with integrated analytics and managed services are positioned to benefit from this transition.
